  {
    "episode_id": 92921,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92921.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "Well, every member of Congress brings his or her own perspective and experiences to the job. And I think one of the great things about our system, it can be frustrating, but one of the great things is there are lots of different perspectives walking into the building on any given day. I'll bring one that's a little different. I think there are quite a few members with state legislative experience, both current members and new members. But as I said, I think I'll be the only former state attorney general in the house. And so that combination is perhaps very helpful. I like to think it'll be helpful in terms of just understanding the basics, the rules, the building, the players, the scope, the basket of issues, different federally than they are at the state level. But the basics of human interaction and trying to get people who really don't agree to come to an agreement is about the same. So too, in terms of what gets written on paper, has consequences downstream as it's implemented, either in terms of how the bureaucracy reads it, or in terms of how court reads it, or in terms of how the regulated parties read it. And so I think those are going to be very helpful experiences coming to the job. Human relations are human relations, and that's true in legislative bodies, whether it's federal or state, and the dynamics were sometimes difficult when I served in the Kansas legislature, as they always are in a legislature, and they're going to be difficult in Washington as well. One of the big differences is we had a large majority in Kansas, but divided government. when I was serving as the Republican leader, the floor leader in the House and the Senate. I'm getting used to saying House. I've said Senate for a very long time. And here we'll have a very slim majority in the House. At some points it may be down to one vote and that's difficult, holding everybody together. But the flip side of that is we'll have unified government with Republican control in all three areas. And so it's different in that sense. And as I've watched the news coverage and talked with folks watching the current leadership try to close out the current Congress. Obviously as we record this they're still in session",
