  {
    "episode_id": 92921,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92921.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "think you're going to see a lot of different pieces in play at once, a little context on this. I mean, Americans are a nation of immigrants. We've always had lots of immigration. We should have lots of immigration. There was a New York Times report this past week that I thought it was not surprising. We knew this, but I thought it was refreshing to see the Times print this, that Over the last three and a half years, we have witnessed the largest migration to the United States in the history of this country, larger than the migration at the end of the 19th century, larger than the migration at the early part of the 20th century, and it's larger both in terms of the raw numbers and in terms of the proportion of the American population that is now matched with migrants. There's nothing inherently bad or good about that. But it is a phenomenon that I think we have to acknowledge it is different. It's not just more the same that we've seen since the 40s or 50s or whatever it may be. In addition, the Times reported, they didn't lead with this, but they did report it that about 60% of those who have come here in the last three and a half years came here unlawfully. let that number sink in. More than half of the people in the largest wave of migration to this country in our history came here in violation of our laws. So it is a different problem than just the sort of ongoing traditional dynamics of managing migration to a nation of immigrants. And I think that's why you've seen so much public reaction to it on both sides, but especially the negative reaction that obviously President Trump and many of us were able to turn into a discussion on the campaign trail. People might not know those numbers. But they get that something's going on here that is different from what we've become accustomed to. So you ask what we're going to do about it. Obviously we're going to let the President set the tone on that. He campaigned on it. Whether you like Donald Trump or don't like Donald Trump, he was very clear that this is a priority issue for him and he's going to set the agenda. We'll react to it and I hope implement most if not all of it. But I do think we should expect to see a focus on those who clearly weren't supposed to be here in the first place, have had their day in court or have violated our laws while here, other than our immigration laws, and need to be removed from the country. There are about, depending on whose numbers you believe, somewhere between 1.3 and about 1.6 million people in this country today, who have either already been convicted of a deportable felony, or have already had their full due process and have been ordered by an immigration judge to leave the country. Yet they're still here. Think about that. That's half the population of Kansas who are not only not supposed to be here, but it's a bright line. There's no question. They've either been ordered out of the country by a court or they've committed a major felony and been convicted of it that's supposed to get removed. That would be a very good place to start, in my view. Now, perhaps not surprising as a former Attorney General. I'm especially concerned about that population. But I do think that's an area we ought to find some agreement in terms of the removal or the deportation piece. And I hope we can find that agreement before we get to the parts where there's not going to be",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
