  {
    "episode_id": 92921,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92921.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 61,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, the whole regulatory reform basket is something I've focused on a lot. As attorney general, we were that AG generation that was dubbed the last line of defense and the Obama administration sued the Biden administration, sometimes over federal laws, but more often over federal regulations, actions by different agencies. On the agency side, we won more than we lost. A little harder to challenge a law enacted by Congress, but agencies have been, I would argue, as a group, largely out of control in the modern history of the United States. A couple of thoughts on that. One, I think we have a moment for meaningful regulatory reform at the federal level. You have a series of Supreme Court decisions that have made the administrative states life more difficult, have really put some walls around agency discretion. And by the way, it's not just the agencies that are to blame here. Congress has developed an unfortunate habit of passing laws that are extraordinarily vague because they can't get agreement in Congress and they can't pass something otherwise. And so they paper over it with these vague laws until the bureaucrats, now you go write the law essentially. And, you know, we are now decades, perhaps even generations into that and it's become addictive to Congress. And so the problem gets worse and worse. And the fixes to it are not going to be headline grabbing. That's part of the challenge. I mean, you can always point to this regulation of that and you can say it's terrible and we need to do away with it, which, you know, there's merit in that. But that's swatting flies. It's systemic procedural reform on both writing more precise laws that delegate less discretion to unelected officials. And then I would argue there may be some changes we need, for example, in the Administrative Procedures Act or the Congressional Review Act, both of which are mind-numbingly dull tools that only insiders worry about. But they are the rule books for how agencies essentially legislate, how they exercise power that Congress has granted to them in sweeping form. I'll give you a little stat that I came across the other day, and I find it shocking. I don't know if others will. Justice Gorsuch has a fairly new book out. It's called Overruled. He argues the threat, the risk of too much law in a free society, including too much regulatory law. And he has a chart in there, a graph in there that I'd never seen before, but it really struck me. It's the number of pages in the federal register, which of course is the publication of all agency rules and regulations that comes out every so often. The number of pages in the federal register has tripled in my lifetime. tripled. That is all agency made law. It is tripled in 56 years. That's not the way this system is supposed to work. And by the way, contrary to what we sometimes say to each other as we're bemoaning Congress, we are not at a moment in American history where Congress is enacting less law. Despite the dysfunction in Washington, despite the inability to get lots of popular things done, The volume of enactments is way up. Gorsuch points out that we're adding something like a million words per year to the United States code. The reason that's happening is that the length, the size of bills is increasing. So even though Congress passes far fewer bills, The volume of pages that it's enacting and writing into federal law has exploded. Obamacare was 1,000 plus pages. All these big reconciliation bills like the inflation reduction act, you know, there are hundreds and hundreds of pages. And by contrast, don't hold me to the number here, but he compares it to the Civil Rights Acts in 64 and 65, which were like 25 pages",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
