    "turn_idx": 65,
    "turn_text": "Well, sure, I think about that with great frequency. I mean, I've generally, as you know, been a free trade advocate throughout my time in public service. I do think that the president-elect has tapped into something that's real, though. And I think in some ways, we're at this point in terms of the American trade agenda because we didn't deal with some of these issues earlier. I'll give you a real example of what I'm talking about here. You know, in the southeast Kansas guy, we have, for most of my adult life, been a somewhat economically depressed region compared with other parts of the state. We were a region that, when we boomed 120 years ago or long while ago, it was largely, it was originally extracted with a lot of natural resource wealth, but then it was manufacturing, a lot of small light manufacturing. in our area still do, but we had a lot when I was growing up. I remember NAFTA in the early 90s. I remember a particular manufacturing plant in my hometown. I remember some of the people who worked there, some of whom I knew very well, and honestly it was the best job some of them had ever had. It was stable. It was reliable. It allowed them to live a predictable and secure life without they weren't getting rich. They were manufacturing car parts. NAFTA shipped that manufacturing plant just south of the Mexican border. And a number of those people never did have stable work again. I'm not saying all of them. Some of them found trade adjustment assistance, some of them found other jobs, and some of them just never found that opportunity again, thinking of one person, one woman in particular, who didn't, and I knew her pretty well. And to say to those folks, you know what? The economists are right. A rising tide floats all boats. And yeah, you paid a price here. But overall, this was good for the country. It was good for your neighbors. It was good for the United States. That is not much of an answer. It might be true. But it's not much of an answer to that person's life circumstance. And I really do think that's what President Trump tapped into, both in his first campaign and then in this campaign as well. That sense of the forgotten man or forgotten America, whatever the phrase is, it's kind of been popularized. But people who felt like nobody cared what government policy was doing to them, trade policy. So all of that's my way of saying, You know, in the first Trump administration, yes, the President levied some tariffs, and yes, there was a price paid for those, but overall, he was pretty targeted, and he was pretty effective in trying to change the behavior of some of our trading partners with tariffs that were much more targeted than the rhetoric was. I'm hopeful he's going to be similarly strategic in this effort. And, you know, we'll see.",
