  {
    "episode_id": 92921,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92921.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think it's unfortunate that politics and the judicial system have somewhat begun to overlap. I mean, obviously, the president's adversaries went after him with multiple investigations and lawsuits using the powers of justice that were at their disposal, both in the states and at the federal level. Over the past four years, I believe he survived those, and I think most of them were unfairly brought. I'm thinking of one of the New York cases, for example, that you can always make a ticky tacky case about the details of the law, but you look at the bigger picture when you're filing a criminal case about is this just compared to how we would treat other defendants, and at least one of those cases was brought, would never have been brought against anybody whose last name wasn't Trump. So it's not as if we're at the start of this problem. I think what I would like to see is the rule of law restored where nobody is above the law and people who are in a position of public trust who break the law are held accountable for whatever their breach may be. At the same time that we don't succumb any further to this temptation to transform the law into politics by other means. That is not the hallmark of American greatness.",
